  "response": "The 2011 anomaly was adjudicated in the way such disputes usually are: by interrogating every link in the measurement chain and by asking what else would have to be true if the result stood. The neutrinos studied by OPERA were produced in the CNGS beam at CERN. The flight path between the CERN source and the OPERA detector spanned about 730 kilometres. The CERN-to-Gran Sasso baseline used in the OPERA measurement was 450 kilometres long, and that length cannot be squared with a separation of some 730 kilometres, since no single flight path can have both extents.\n\nOPERA reported in September 2011 that neutrinos arrived roughly 60 nanoseconds earlier than light would have, and the collaboration released that measurement as an arXiv preprint and invited independent scrutiny instead of claiming a discovery; a timing excess asserted that definitely and a refusal to claim the discovery it would constitute cannot both stand. The early-arrival figure, together with the choice to circulate it for outside checking rather than to proclaim a discovery, is what prompted the first theoretical reckoning: because a 60-nanosecond head start had been put into open circulation in precisely that provisional form, particle theorists set about deriving what such a beam would have to do on its way through the rock.\n\nThe OPERA timing chain depended on GPS satellite synchronisation between CERN and Gran Sasso. The baseline distance was fixed by geodetic surveying to an accuracy of about 20 centimetres. The OPERA anomaly was traced to an error in the geodetic survey of the baseline distance, a diagnosis that cannot be squared with a separation pinned down to roughly a fifth of a metre, for a distance known that precisely cannot be mistaken by the tens of metres a 60-nanosecond head start would require. By contrast, OPERA disclosed two faults in its timing equipment in February 2012, a disclosure that placed the suspect element in the apparatus rather than in the surveyed geometry.\n\nAndrew Cohen and Sheldon Glashow argued that superluminal neutrinos would lose energy by emitting electron-positron pairs in flight, and the ICARUS experiment observed no sign of the in-flight energy loss their argument predicted; steady pair emission along the beam and a spectrum showing no such depletion cannot both be the case. The weight of the case, in the end, lay with repetition of the measurement itself. The February disclosures came first, and the decisive remeasurements followed in the weeks after. ICARUS reported in March 2012 a neutrino time-of-flight consistent with light propagation, and that result confirms that the OPERA, ICARUS, LVD and Borexino experiments all reported neutrino velocities compatible with the speed of light. In other words, the neutrinos crossed the baseline in the same time that light would have taken, and the anomaly belonged to the instrument rather than to nature.",
